# Migrating `EventProcessor` to .NET 8

## Key Problems with the Original Code

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** in .NET 8 — it was removed entirely.
2. **`Thread.Interrupt()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** in .NET 8 as well.
3. **Race condition**: Multiple enqueues between `WaitOne()` and `Reset()` lose items (only one dequeue per signal).
4. **No graceful cancellation** mechanism.

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private Task? _processingTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _processingTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken ct)
    {
        try
        {
            await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(ct))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // Expected on shutdown
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        if (!_channel.Writer.TryWrite(item))
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Channel closed.");
    }

    public async ValueTask StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();           // Signal no more items
        _cts?.Cancel();                       // Cancel if stuck waiting

        if (_processingTask is not null)
        {
            using var timeoutCts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
            try
            {
                await _processingTask.WaitAsync(timeoutCts.Token);
            }
            catch (TimeoutException)
            {
                // Task didn't finish in time — it's abandoned but not forcefully killed
            }
        }

        _cts?.Dispose();
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync() => await StopAsync();

    private void Handle(string item)
    {
        // Your processing logic here
    }
}
```

## Migration Map

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 Replacement |
|---|---|
| `ManualResetEvent` + `Queue` + `lock` | `Channel<T>` (thread-safe, async-ready) |
| `Thread` + manual loop | `Task.Run` + `await foreach` |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | `CancellationToken` |
| `Thread.Abort()` | Cooperative cancellation (no equivalent) |
| `WaitOne()` blocking | `ReadAllAsync()` awaits asynchronously |

## Critical Pitfalls

1. **No forced termination** — .NET 8 has no `Abort()`. If `Handle()` can hang, you must make it respect a `CancellationToken` or implement your own timeout logic inside it.

2. **Lost-item race condition fixed** — `Channel<T>` internally queues all items; no signal/reset race.

3. **Bounded vs Unbounded** — Use `Channel.CreateBounded<string>(capacity)` if you need backpressure to prevent unbounded memory growth.

4. **Synchronous callers** — If you can't use `async` at the call site for `Stop()`:
   ```csharp
   public void Stop() => StopAsync().AsTask().GetAwaiter().GetResult();
   ```
   But prefer async all the way up.

5. **Exception handling** — If `Handle()` throws, the loop dies silently. Add a try/catch inside the loop body or use a fault-tolerant wrapper:
   ```csharp
   await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(ct))
   {
       try { Handle(item); }
       catch (Exception ex) { Log(ex); }
   }
   ```

6. **`Thread.IsBackground`** — The original code's thread could keep the process alive. `Task.Run` uses thread-pool threads (background by default), so the app exits cleanly.
